    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The causes of atopic dermatitis (AD), or eczema, are currently unknown. However, it is thought that there are immunomodulatory pathways that affect skin barrier function that can lead to the development of AD, and that the disease can be exacerbated by colonization of the skin with the bacterium, Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus). Here, we exploit a model involving genetically-modified mice (“Rabgef1KERT2-KO” mice) in which treatment of the skin with tamoxifen can result in depletion of keratinocyte levels of RAB guanine nucleotide exchange factor (RabGEF1, also known as RABEX-5). Upon repetitive tamoxifen treatment, these mice develop skin pathology with many of the features of AD. Using this model, we investigated whether a depletion of keratinocyte RabGEF1 influenced colonization of the skin with S. aureus and whether such skin tissue exhibited changes in levels of antimicrobial activity. We found that the skin of tamoxifen-treated RabGEF1-depleted mice exhibited increased growth of S. aureus as well as stronger antimicrobial properties and increased epidermal thickness compared to identically-treated control (RabGEF1-sufficient) mice. In a preliminary experiment, we also found that there were higher numbers of S. aureus in the skin of RabGEF1-depleted mice which also were deficient in RIP3, a protein kinase that is required for programmed necroptosis. These results suggest that RabGEF1 and RIP3 are host factors which can importantly influence both the skin pathology associated with this AD model and also the extent of S. aureus colonization of the animals’ AD-like skin lesions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
